For ready reference, Section 7 of the Act, 1948 is hereby reproduced as under:-
"[7A. General duties of the occupier.--(1) Every occupier shall ensure, so far as is reasonably practicable, the health, safety and welfare of all workers while they are at work in the factory.
(2) Without prejudice to the generality of the provisions of sub-section (1), the matters to which such duty extends, shall include-- (a) the provision and maintenance of plant and systems of work in the factory that are safe and without risks to health;
(b) the arrangements in the factory for ensuring safety and absence of risks to health in connection with the use, handling, storage and transport of articles and substances;
(c) the provisions of such information, instruction, training and supervision as are necessary to ensure the health and safety of all workers at work;

The LOTO procedure was rolled out to prevent man-machine interfaces on running machines and all persons engaged in Plants were trained on these standards;
(iv) that after clearing the Crusher jam, as per the crusher inspection standard practices, only the rear window i.e. the inspection window was left open to observe and check the Coal Crusher's performance during start-up;
(v) that subsequently, the work permits were returned and the deceased opened his LOTO lock;
(vi) that after returning the LOTO, the Sr. Engineer Mr. Tiwari and Workman, Mr. Raj Kumar Mishra specifically instructed the deceased workman to observe the crusher from a safe distance of 2 meters;
(vii) that the deceased, out of his own volition, decided to use the crowbar to poke into the grizzly section of the crusher through the rear inspection Window, and that it was surmised that, in doing so, the crow bar may have recoiled from the impact of one of the Crusher hammers and the other end of the Crowbar hit the deceased on his head, causing a 5 to 6 cm, fatal wound above his left ear;
(viii) that the deceased had been issued PPE like helmet, protective eye glasses, safety shoes,
reflector jackets etc., which he was wearing before commencing his unwarranted poking activity;
(ix) that the deceased was an employee in Kymore Cement Works for 4 years and was tasked with the job of a Helper in the coal handling plant throughout his association with ACC;
(x) that he was given safety training as required under the Factories Act, 1948 and had passed the OHS Refresher Training Program;
(xi) that ACC Management had fulfilled their safety obligations under the applicable law and that the unfortunate incident could be attributed solely to the unsafe, unauthorized and unwarranted acts of the deceased;
(xii) that in light of the aforesaid, there was no contravention of the Factories Act or the rules framed thereunder; and (xiii) that the ACC Management had nevertheless provided interim relief to the next of kin of the deceased and that process under the Workmen's Compensation Act, 1923 had already been initiated.
